window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"10-04","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"10-04-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-04-01/","title_he":"א – מצוות ברכת המזון","title_en":"The Mitzva of Birkat Ha-mazon","body_en":"<p>It is a Torah commandment that after a person eats bread and is satisfied, he must bless God, as it is stated (Devarim 8:10): \"You shall eat and be satisfied, and you shall bless the Lord your God for the good land that He has given you.\" The Sages learned that Birkat Ha-mazon must include three matters: from the words \"and you shall bless the Lord your God,\" they learned that one must recite the blessing of Ha-zan, in which we bless God for the food. From the words \"for the land,\" they learned that one must bless and give thanks for the land. And from the fact that the Torah went on to say \"for the <b>good</b> land that He has given you,\" they learned that one must bless for what is good and choicest in the land, namely Jerusalem (Berakhot 48b).</p><p>The Torah commandment is that the one reciting the blessing mention these matters in any wording he wishes, according to his understanding and his power of expression. Then Moshe, Yehoshua, David and Shlomo came and instituted a choice and complete wording, according to the good that had been added in their days. When the manna came down for Israel, Moshe Rabbeinu instituted the wording of the blessing of Ha-zan, and no wording had yet been fixed for the blessing over the good land. When Israel entered the Land, Yehoshua bin Nun instituted the wording of the blessing of the land, and no wording had yet been fixed for the blessing over the good and choicest place in the land. When King David established Jerusalem as the city of holiness and of the kingdom, he instituted the wording of the blessing \"for Israel Your people and for Jerusalem Your city,\" and when King Shlomo built the Holy Temple, he added \"for the great and holy House.\" While the Temple stood, they would ask that God continue its existence, and after it was destroyed, they instituted a wording in which we ask God to have mercy on us, to build Jerusalem, to restore the kingship of the House of David to its place and to build the Temple (Berakhot 48b; Rashba; Ramban, as cited in BY 187:1; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 187:1-3).</p><p>The Sages said (Berakhot 48b; SA 187:2-3) that it is obligatory to mention in the blessing of the land the praise of the land, that it is a desirable, good and spacious land. One must also mention the covenant of circumcision (brit mila) and the Torah, because it is through these two mitzvot that we inherit the land. As was said to Avraham Avinu when he was commanded regarding the covenant of circumcision (Bereishit 17:7-8): \"I will establish My <b>covenant</b> between Me and you and your descendants after you throughout their generations as an everlasting <b>covenant</b>, to be God to you and to your descendants after you. And I will give to you and to your descendants after you the land of your sojournings, all the land of Canaan, as an everlasting possession, and I will be their God.\"</p><p>We have likewise learned that it is by virtue of the Torah and the mitzvot that we inherit the land, as it is stated at the beginning of the passage in which we were commanded regarding Birkat Ha-mazon (Devarim 8:1): \"All the commandment that I command you this day you shall observe to do, that you may live and multiply, and go in and inherit the land that the Lord swore to your fathers.\" And it is also stated (Tehillim 105:44-45): \"He gave them the lands of the nations, and they inherited the toil of peoples, so that they might keep His statutes and observe His teachings. Halleluya.\"</p><p>The Sages further said that the mention of the covenant must precede the mention of the Torah, because in connection with the covenant of circumcision thirteen covenants are mentioned, whereas over the Torah only three covenants were made. The covenant of circumcision expresses the intrinsic holiness with which God sanctified His people Israel, a holiness that does not depend on our choice, and it is the foundation for receiving the Torah, which demands of us that we choose the good.</p><p>In the third blessing they instituted mentioning the kingship of the House of David, because it was through David that Jerusalem was sanctified, and the essential settlement of the land and of Jerusalem depends on the kingship of Israel (Berakhot 48b; SA 188:3).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-04-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-04-02/","title_he":"ב – ברכת הטוב והמטיב","title_en":"The Blessing of Ha-tov Ve-hametiv","body_en":"<p>After the destruction of the Temple and the devastation of the land, it seemed as though there was no longer any hope for the rebuilding of the land and of Jerusalem, and no longer any point in continuing to bless God \"for the good land.\" Therefore they added and instituted the blessing of Ha-tov Ve-hametiv (\"who is good and does good\"), in order to strengthen the faith that everything is for the good, and that even the destruction, the exile and the suffering are meant to rectify us and to bring us anew to a more complete rebuilding of the land, Jerusalem and the Temple.</p><p>The Sages in Yavne instituted the blessing on a special day, the day on which those killed at Beitar were given over for burial. Beitar was a large city in Judea, and it was the capital of Bar Kokhba, and from it they hoped to restore Israel's independence and the building of the Temple. When it fell and was destroyed, the horn of Israel was cut down and the troubles grew very great, and the wicked kingdom decreed that Jerusalem be plowed over and that the names of the land and of Jerusalem be changed, in order to wipe out their memory from the world. They further decreed that those killed at Beitar not be buried. Some time later, when a new king was appointed, permission was given to bury the slain, and it was found that a miracle had been done for them and they had not decomposed. The Sages said (Berakhot 48b): \"On the day that those killed at Beitar were given over for burial, they instituted Ha-tov Ve-hametiv in Yavne. 'Ha-tov' (who is good) – that they did not decompose; and 'Ve-hametiv' (and does good) – that they were given over for burial.\" Our master Rav Kook explained (Ein Aya there) that from the fact that they did not decompose we learn that the destruction and the exile did not nullify the essential selfhood of Israel. And in the fact that they were given over for burial there is a hint of the promise that they will yet rise to their destiny at the end of days, and this is a sign of the fulfillment of all the prophets' promises of revival and redemption.</p><p>Since the obligation to recite it is different – the first three blessings being from the Torah while this one is rabbinic – therefore, upon completing the first three blessings, the one reciting the blessing answers \"Amen\" after his own blessing: \"who builds Jerusalem [in His mercy] – Amen\" (SA 188:1).</p><p>And since the series of Torah blessings concludes at the end of the third blessing, the fourth blessing, which the Sages instituted, is a blessing in its own right, and therefore it opens with \"Barukh\" (\"Blessed\"). For this is the rule: when a blessing is adjacent to another, it relies on it and does not need to open with \"Barukh\"; but when it is a blessing that stands on its own, it must open with \"Barukh.\"<sup>1</sup></p><p>The Sages further said (Berakhot 46a) that the guest should bless the host after Birkat Ha-mazon, and this is the wording of the blessing (SA 201:1): \"May it be His will that this host not be shamed or disgraced, neither in this world nor in the World to Come; may he succeed in all his dealings, and may his possessions be successful and close to the city; may Satan have no power over the work of his hands, and may no matter of sin or thought of iniquity come before him, from now and forever.\" One may lengthen and expand this wording, and this is the practice of those of Sephardic descent. Many of those of Ashkenazic descent are accustomed to make do with a short wording: \"May the Merciful One bless the master of this house.\" But ideally it is proper to say the wording whose basis is in the Gemara (MB 201:5; and below 5:4).</p><p>Since we have already merited to be sanctified through the mitzva of Birkat Ha-mazon, Israel has been accustomed to add personal requests that open with \"Ha-raḥaman\" (\"May the Merciful One\"), as well as other general prayers for the redemption. And although this is not an enactment of the Sages, since this has been the practice since the days of the Rishonim, the custom is binding, each community according to its custom. However, if a person needs to interrupt by speaking or to go somewhere, since he has finished the fourth blessing he may interrupt, and afterward, when he is able, he should complete what he omitted (see below, note 13).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"Since the blessing of Ha-tov Ve-hametiv is a blessing that stands on its own, one must mention God's kingship in it, for so the Sages instituted, that in every blessing one mention the name of God and His kingship, and if one did not mention the Name or kingship in a blessing, it is not a blessing (SA 214:1). Therefore, at the opening of the blessing of Ha-tov Ve-hametiv we say: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God (the name of God), King of the universe (kingship).\" But in the second and third blessings kingship is not mentioned, because they are a continuation of the first, and both rely on the mention of kingship in the first blessing. This is like the Shemoneh Esrei prayer, in which God's kingship is mentioned only in the first blessing, and afterward all the blessings rely on it (Tosafot Berakhot 40b, s.v. \"amar\"). The Sages further said (Berakhot 49a) that one should mention kingship an additional time in the blessing of Ha-tov Ve-hametiv, because after we have mentioned the kingship of the House of David in the third blessing, it would have been fitting to mention the kingship of Heaven as well, but it is not fitting to mention the kingship of Heaven adjacent to the kingship of flesh and blood; therefore the mention of kingship that should have been said in the third blessing is completed in the blessing of Ha-tov Ve-hametiv. And since this is so, one adds yet another mention of kingship, on behalf of the second blessing. The three mentions of kingship are: 1) \"King of the universe,\" 2) \"our King, our Mighty One,\" 3) \"the King who is good and does good to all.\" And since kingship is said three times, three acts of goodness and three bestowals are also said in it: \"He has done good, He does good, He will do good to us; He has bestowed upon us, He bestows upon us, He will bestow upon us forever\" (Tur and SA 189:1; MB 4)."}]},{"slug":"10-04-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-04-03/","title_he":"ג – משמעות ברכת הזן","title_en":"The Meaning of the Blessing of Ha-zan","body_en":"<p>In order to delve into the subject of Birkat Ha-mazon, one must contemplate the entire passage at whose center we were commanded regarding Birkat Ha-mazon (Devarim 8:1-18). The first verse presents the general aim: \"All the commandment that I command you this day you shall observe to do, that you may live and multiply, and go in and inherit the land that the Lord swore to your fathers.\" Afterward two central foundations are explained: the first, that God feeds everything, as we say in the first blessing; the second, the praise of the Land of Israel, as we say in the second and third blessings. We will focus first on the foundations connected to the first blessing, the blessing of Ha-zan (\"who feeds\").</p><p>Many times, when a person's livelihood is readily available to him, he forgets that God watches over him and provides for him, and therefore the Torah instructed (ibid. 8:2): \"You shall remember all the way that the Lord your God led you these forty years in the wilderness, in order to afflict you, to test you, to know what was in your heart, whether you would keep His commandments or not.\" Life in the wilderness was hard; out of the many hardships one could rail against Heaven, and on the other hand, one could strengthen oneself in faith and contemplate the miracles through which the Holy One, blessed be He, sustains Israel. The most difficult test was connected to eating the manna. A person is accustomed to worry about tomorrow; for this purpose he prepares his food for himself through his work in the field, in industry and in commerce. But all this was not possible in the wilderness. It was forbidden even to keep manna from one day to the next, and it was not even possible to eat more than a person needed, for the amount of manna was limited to what a person needed to eat in one day and no more. Every Jew had to believe in God that tomorrow too He would send down manna for him from heaven. And whoever weakened in his faith felt hunger, as it is stated (ibid. 3-4): \"He afflicted you and let you hunger, and fed you the manna, which you did not know, nor did your fathers know.\"</p><p>Through this test it was engraved in our national consciousness that nature does not exist by itself, but rather God is the one who provides for everything – \"in order to make you know that man does not live by bread alone, but by everything that comes out of the mouth of the Lord does man live. Your clothing did not wear out upon you, nor did your foot swell, these forty years.\" These were the first forty years of the appearance of the people of Israel in the world, years in which it learned and internalized the values of the Torah. Through the miraculous life in the wilderness we learned well that everything comes from God in order to do good for us, and even if He withholds His blessing from us for a certain period, this is only out of His love for us, in order to set us on the path of truth, as it is stated (ibid. 5-6): \"You shall know in your heart that just as a man disciplines his son, so the Lord your God disciplines you. And you shall keep the commandments of the Lord your God, to walk in His ways and to fear Him.\"</p><p>The essence of the mitzva is to bless God when a person has eaten and is satisfied, as it is stated (Devarim 8:10): \"You shall eat and be satisfied, and you shall bless the Lord your God for the good land that He has given you.\" For when a person is satiated he is liable to become arrogant and forget that everything comes from God, and about this the Torah goes on to warn (ibid. 11-16): \"Beware lest you forget the Lord your God by not keeping His commandments, His ordinances and His statutes, which I command you this day; lest you eat and be satisfied, and build good houses and dwell in them; and your herds and your flocks multiply, and your silver and gold multiply, and all that you have multiply; and your heart be lifted up, and you forget the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage; who led you through the great and terrible wilderness, of fiery serpents and scorpions and thirsty ground where there was no water; who brought forth water for you out of the flinty rock; who fed you in the wilderness with manna, which your fathers did not know, in order to afflict you and in order to test you, to do you good in your end.\"</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-04-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-04-04/","title_he":"ד – מרכזיותה של ברכת הארץ","title_en":"The Centrality of the Blessing of the Land","body_en":"<p>The second foundation that runs through the entire passage of Birkat Ha-mazon is the praise of the Land of Israel. The Sages said (Bamidbar Rabba 23:7) about the blessings in Birkat Ha-mazon: \"None of them is more beloved than the blessing 'for the land.'\" And this is the plain meaning of the verse, that the main aim is to bless God for the good land, as it is stated (Devarim 8:10): \"You shall eat and be satisfied, and you shall bless the Lord your God for the good land that He has given you.\" The third blessing, over Jerusalem, is also a continuation of the blessing of the land, for Jerusalem is the heart of \"the good land.\" We likewise learned from the verse that opens the passage that the main fulfillment of the mitzvot is specifically in the Land of Israel, as it is stated (Devarim 8:1): \"All the commandment that I command you this day you shall observe to do, that you may live and multiply, and go in and inherit the land that the Lord swore to your fathers.\"</p><p>The explanation for this is that the main purpose of Israel is to reveal the word of God in the world while grappling with the complications of earthly life. Therefore the situation in which we are fed by manna that came down from heaven is temporary, in order to acquire the basic awareness that God is the one who feeds everything. But the aim is that man be a partner with the Holy One, blessed be He, in perfecting the world, by bringing forth his food from the earth through plowing, sowing, planting and raising animals. Through this he will reveal the Divine image within him, bring out the good in earthliness, and rise to a connection full of life with God. Indeed, the manna that came down from heaven had no waste, and it was entirely absorbed into the limbs, without their needing to relieve themselves after eating it. But man's main destiny is to eat physical foods, which contain waste, yet on the other hand the good in them is more beloved, and holier sparks are hidden within them. For this purpose we were given the Land of Israel, which is the Holy Land; although it is a physical land, it is holy, and the fruits that grow in it are holy.</p><p>Many tend to think that the further a person distances himself from physicality and from sensing the various tastes in foods, the purer and holier he will be. But the Torah teaches us that in the Land of Israel there is great value to physical abundance, to varied food and its excellent taste, which too was created by the Holy One, blessed be He, in order to bring us to our completeness. Therefore the Torah takes the trouble to praise before us the goodness and desirability of the Land of Israel, as it is stated (ibid. 7-9): \"For the Lord your God is bringing you into a good land, a land of brooks of water, of springs and depths flowing forth in valley and hill; a land of wheat and barley, and vine and fig and pomegranate; a land of olive oil and honey; a land in which you will eat bread without scarcity, you will lack nothing in it; a land whose stones are iron and from whose hills you will dig copper.\" And therefore we were commanded to bless God for this, as it is stated (Devarim 8:10): \"You shall eat and be satisfied, and you shall bless the Lord your God for the good land that He has given you.\"</p><p>In the first blessing we dwell on the foundation that God is the one \"who feeds all,\" and just as He gave us the manna in the wilderness miraculously, so He always sustains nature; thus, the root of all the food we eat is from Him, may He be blessed, alone. In the second blessing we bless God for granting us the ability to be partners in perfecting the world and in bringing forth food from the earth, and therefore the blessing concludes: \"for the land and for the food.\"<sup>2</sup></p><p>However, there is a concern lest success in building the land drown a person in desire and arrogance and make him forget his destiny. And about this we were commanded (Devarim 8:11-18): \"Beware lest you forget the Lord your God by not keeping His commandments, His ordinances and His statutes, which I command you this day; lest you eat and be satisfied, and build good houses and dwell in them; and your herds and your flocks multiply, and your silver and gold multiply, and all that you have multiply; and your heart be lifted up, and you forget the Lord your God … and you say in your heart, 'My power and the might of my hand have gotten me this wealth.' But you shall remember the Lord your God, for it is He who gives you power to get wealth, in order to establish His covenant which He swore to your fathers, as it is this day.\"</p><p>In Birkat Ha-mazon we remind ourselves again that God is the one who gave us the food and the land, and through this we will be roused to understand that the strength, the satisfaction and the joy that we received from the food are meant to sanctify us and to elevate us.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"The first blessing is over food that involves no refining (berurim) and elevation of sparks; the second blessing is over food that involves refining and elevation of sparks; and the third blessing is over the completion of the elevation of the sparks, in the aspect of Jerusalem and the Temple. The ability to carry out the refining in the Land of Israel comes through covenant and Torah. See an expanded treatment of these matters in Likkutei Torah of the Admor Ha-zaken, Ekev, pp. 14-17."}]},{"slug":"10-04-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-04-05/","title_he":"ה – דיני אמירת ברכת המזון וחינוך הקטנים","title_en":"Laws of Reciting Birkat Ha-mazon and Educating Children","body_en":"<p>The one reciting the blessing must make audible to his own ear what he utters with his mouth, and if he did not articulate the words with his lips, he has not fulfilled his obligation. Ideally, one should say the blessing aloud, because the voice arouses concentration. One who does not understand Hebrew may say the blessing in translation into a language he understands (SA 185:1-3; MB 1-3; and above 1:6-7).</p><p>After the fact, one who omitted passages from Birkat Ha-mazon has fulfilled his obligation, as long as he mentioned the foundations that must be in Birkat Ha-mazon. These are the foundations: to bless God for the food, and within the blessing to mention His name and that He is King of the universe (SA 187:1; MB 4); in the second blessing, to thank God for the land, to mention that it is desirable, good and spacious, and to mention the covenant of circumcision and the study of Torah; in the third blessing, to mention Jerusalem, the Temple and the kingship of the House of David; and in the fourth blessing, to say its essence.</p><p>A child who has reached the age of education (ḥinukh), that is, the age at which he understands in a general way the matter of blessings, it is a mitzva to train him to recite all the blessings, and foremost among them Birkat Ha-mazon, which is the most important of the blessings. Most children reach the age of education at around six. Before that, when they understand a little of how to bless, it is good to begin teaching them to bless, but they are not yet obligated to bless. Even children who have already reached the age of education, when it is hard for them to recite all of Birkat Ha-mazon, should be accustomed to say the first blessing, and in this way they will progress according to their ability.<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"In the introduction of the Magen Avraham to siman 192, a short wording of the essence of Birkat Ha-mazon is cited, and Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 187:4 and MB 4 wrote that when necessary one may train children to bless with the abbreviated wording. However, the custom is to train children to say the regular wording gradually.<br><b>Is the obligation of the three blessings from the Torah, and is there a connection between them?</b> According to Tosafot (Berakhot 16a), their obligation is from the Torah, and the Sages were lenient with workers to recite only two, because the Sages have the power to uproot something from the Torah. According to the Rambam, from the Torah there is no set number of blessings; rather, one must mention the three foundations, which are a blessing over the food, the praise of the land, and Jerusalem, and therefore workers can include all the foundations in two blessings (BY, SA 191. In practice, today workers are obligated in all four blessings).<br>One who ate, and after finishing his meal realized that he has no bentcher (birkon), and he knows by heart only one or two of the blessings of Birkat Ha-mazon, and of the others he does not know even their essential, indispensable content: according to the Magen Avraham, all three blessings are a single unit, and whoever cannot say all of them should not say even one. According to the Even Ha-ozer and other poskim, he should say the blessing that he knows. In practice, if he ate and was satisfied, he is obligated by Torah law and should be stringent and say the blessing he knows by heart. If he was not satisfied, his obligation is rabbinic, and in cases of doubt we are lenient; therefore, as long as he does not know the three blessings whose obligation is from the Torah, he should not bless at all (Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav; MB 194:13; Sha'ar Ha-tziyun there). But if it is the fourth blessing that he does not know, even if he was not satisfied, he should say the first three, whose obligation is from the Torah (SA 194:3). The order of the blessings is indispensable (Responsa Devar Shmuel 147; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 187:2).<br>If instead of reciting Birkat Ha-mazon one recited Me'ein Shalosh (the abridged three-in-one blessing), the poskim disagree as to whether he fulfilled his obligation. According to the Ra'ah, the Ḥida and Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 168:8, he has fulfilled it. And although \"covenant,\" \"Torah\" and \"the kingship of the House of David\" are not mentioned in it, since he blessed with a wording the Sages instituted, and it contains all the foundations that must be there by Torah law, after the fact he has fulfilled his obligation. In contrast, according to the Ritva in his Hilkhot Berakhot (2:21), the Baḥ and the Eliya Rabba, he has not fulfilled it. In practice, if he already recited Me'ein Shalosh, he has fulfilled his obligation after the fact. See Yabia Omer 2:12. One who eats bread and does not know Birkat Ha-mazon by heart but knows Al Ha-miḥya: if he was satisfied, he should recite Al Ha-miḥya, and if he was not satisfied, he should not recite Al Ha-miḥya."}]},{"slug":"10-04-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-04-06/","title_he":"ו – החובה מהתורה כששבע ומדברי חכמים בכזית","title_en":"The Torah Obligation When Satisfied, and the Rabbinic Obligation for a Kezayit","body_en":"<p>By Torah law a person is obligated to recite Birkat Ha-mazon when he has eaten a meal with bread and is satisfied, as it is stated (Devarim 8:10): \"You shall eat and be satisfied, and you shall bless.\" The Sages added and instituted that even a person who ate a kezayit of bread, since he derived enjoyment, should recite all of Birkat Ha-mazon. The Sages said (Berakhot 20b) that the ministering angels argued before the Holy One, blessed be He: \"Master of the Universe, it is written in Your Torah (Devarim 10:19): 'who shows no favor and takes no bribe,' yet do You not show favor to Israel, as it is stated (Bamidbar 6:26): 'May the Lord lift up His countenance toward you'? He said to them: And shall I not show favor to Israel? For I wrote for them in the Torah: 'You shall eat and be satisfied, and you shall bless the Lord your God,' and they are exacting with themselves and bless even for a kezayit.\"</p><p>When a person is in doubt whether he recited Birkat Ha-mazon for what he ate: if he was satisfied, since the doubt concerns a Torah commandment, he must be stringent and recite Birkat Ha-mazon. If he was not satisfied, since the doubt concerns a rabbinic commandment, he should not bless (SA 184:4).</p><p>If his friend remembers seeing him bless, he may rely on him (Maharsham 4:29). Likewise, if he himself knows that he is accustomed to bless immediately after eating, even though he does not remember it precisely, he may rely on the presumption (ḥazaka) that he presumably blessed (based on Halakhot Ketanot 2:278; see Kaf Ha-ḥayim 184:21).</p><p>According to most poskim, even if he ate only a kezayit of bread, if he ate other foods as well and from all of them together he became satisfied, he is obligated to recite Birkat Ha-mazon by Torah law.<sup>4</sup></p><p>Of course women too are obligated in Birkat Ha-mazon, but a doubt arose whether their obligation is from the Torah or rabbinic, for perhaps, as in several matters, there is a difference between men and women: with men the emphasis is more on obligation, whereas with women the emphasis is more on volunteering. Indeed, the entire doubt concerns only the question of whether women are obligated in Birkat Ha-mazon by Torah law, but by rabbinic law it is clear that women are obligated like men (SA 186:1; MB 187:9).<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"In the Mishna and Gemara (Berakhot 44a), according to Rabban Gamliel, it is a mitzva to recite Birkat Ha-mazon, which contains three blessings, by Torah law not only over bread but also over any fruit of the seven species, since they were mentioned in the passage in which we were commanded regarding Birkat Ha-mazon, as it is stated (Devarim 8:8-10): \"a land of wheat and barley, and vine and fig and pomegranate; a land of olive oil and honey; a land in which you will eat bread without scarcity, you will lack nothing in it; a land whose stones are iron and from whose hills you will dig copper. You shall eat and be satisfied, and you shall bless the Lord your God for the good land that He has given you.\" And according to Rabbi Akiva, \"even if he ate shelek (a cooked vegetable dish) and it is his staple meal, he recites three blessings over it,\" because the mitzva is that a person recite Birkat Ha-mazon over his meal. The halakha follows the view of the Sages, that only one who eats bread and is satisfied is obligated in Birkat Ha-mazon by Torah law, because in the verse adjacent to the mitzva of Birkat Ha-mazon bread is mentioned, from which satiety principally comes.<br>According to most Rishonim and Aḥaronim, by Torah law only one who was satisfied from a meal that included bread is obligated in Birkat Ha-mazon, as it is stated: \"and be satisfied,\" and it was the Sages who obligated reciting Birkat Ha-mazon for less: according to Rabbi Meir, for eating a kezayit, and according to Rabbi Yehuda, for a kebeitza (Berakhot 49b). So wrote the Behag, Rashi, the Rambam, Rabbeinu Tam, the Rid, the Rosh, the Semag and many others, and they ruled according to Rabbi Meir. So too was it ruled in SA 184:6; see BHL s.v. \"be-khazayit.\" In contrast, some say that since it is stated \"you shall eat,\" even one who was not satisfied is obligated in Birkat Ha-mazon by Torah law (and the Gemara in Berakhot 20b is not followed as halakha). According to the Ramban and the Ra'avad, the Torah obligation begins once one has eaten a kezayit of bread, and according to Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona and Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, one who eats a kebeitza is obligated by Torah law. See below 10, note 1.<br>From many poskim it is implied that the measure of satiety depends on the feeling of the one eating, and so wrote the Sefer Ha-ḥinukh. However, according to Rashi's teachers (Berakhot 42a), even when he was not satisfied, if he ate an amount from which people commonly become satisfied, he is obligated by Torah law (MB 184:22; Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 25).<br>In the plain sense, the other foods eaten at the meal are combined toward the measure of satiety, for otherwise it would often turn out that a person who was satisfied would not be obligated in the blessing by Torah law. So wrote Halakhot Ketanot 2:227 and Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 168:8, that if one ate a kezayit of bread and other foods and was satisfied from all of them together, he is obligated in Birkat Ha-mazon by Torah law. So too wrote Igrot Moshe OḤ 4:41. However, the Pri Megadim 184, Eshel Avraham 8, inclined to explain that only if one was satisfied from the bread alone is he obligated by Torah law. So too wrote the Tzlaḥ and the Pnei Yehoshua. See the end of BHL 184:6, s.v. \"kezayit,\" that if the additional foods are meant to accompany the bread, he is certainly obligated by Torah law. In practice, the view of many Aḥaronim is that if one ate a kezayit of bread and other foods and was satisfied, he is obligated by Torah law (see Ḥazon Ovadia, Berakhot p. 238).<br>According to the Yere'im and several other poskim, one who was thirsty at the meal and did not drink is not obligated by Torah law, because according to Rabbi Meir, \"you shall eat\" means the measure of a kezayit, and \"be satisfied\" refers to drinking. Therefore it is good that one who comes to recite Birkat Ha-mazon not be thirsty, so that he will merit fulfilling a Torah commandment according to all opinions. The Rema 197:3 took his view into account, ruling that ideally one who was thirsty at the meal and did not drink should not discharge the obligation of one who did drink. It is possible that the Yere'im's intent is an intoxicating drink, that one who wanted to drink it at the meal and did not drink is not fully satisfied and is not obligated in Birkat Ha-mazon by Torah law.<br>One who repeats the blessing out of doubt should, according to most poskim, also recite Ha-tov Ve-hametiv (Magen Avraham 184:7; MB 13)."},{"n":5,"en":"In the Gemara (Berakhot 20b) the question was raised whether women are obligated in Birkat Ha-mazon by Torah law like men, or by rabbinic law. The reason that perhaps their obligation is only rabbinic is that the land was not apportioned to women (Rashi), or because the mention of \"covenant\" and \"Torah\" is indispensable (Berakhot 49a), and women are not obligated in the mitzvot of the covenant and Torah study (Tosafot).<br>The Rishonim disagree about the conclusion of the Gemara. According to the Rif, the Ra'avad, the Ramban and others, women are obligated by Torah law. According to the Rambam, the Rosh, Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona and many others, there is a doubt. As for the halakha, when a woman was satisfied and is in doubt whether she recited Birkat Ha-mazon: according to Sha'arei Efrayim, Ḥayei Adam and Magen Giborim, she is obligated to bless. According to Rabbi Akiva Eiger, Birkei Yosef and Yeḥaveh Da'at 6:10, she should not bless. In practice, she is permitted to bless (MB 186:3)."}]},{"slug":"10-04-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-04-07/","title_he":"ז – תוספות בשבתות וחגים","title_en":"Additions on Shabbat and Festivals","body_en":"<p>On holy days on which a Musaf offering was brought, the Sages instituted mentioning the holiness of the day in Birkat Ha-mazon, for eating on these days is not like eating on weekdays; rather, it carries an added measure of mitzva and holiness, and this must be expressed in the blessing. On Shabbat one says \"Retzei Ve-haḥalitzenu\"; and on Yom Tov, Ḥol Ha-mo'ed and Rosh Ḥodesh one says \"Ya'aleh Ve-yavo.\" They instituted saying the addition in the blessing of Boneh Yerushalayim (\"who builds Jerusalem\"), because it contains a plea for mercy, and the mention that the Sages instituted also contains a plea for mercy. In \"Retzei Ve-haḥalitzenu\" we ask that we merit the holiness of the Shabbat day, to cease from labor and rest on it, and to see the consolation of Zion and Jerusalem. And in \"Ya'aleh Ve-yavo\" we ask that God remember us for good, have mercy on us and save us (Berakhot 49a; Shabbat 24a, and Tosafot there).</p><p>When Yom Tov or Rosh Ḥodesh falls on Shabbat, one first says \"Retzei Ve-haḥalitzenu,\" because Shabbat is more frequent and holier, and afterward one says \"Ya'aleh Ve-yavo\" (SA 188:5; MB 13).</p><p>On Ḥanukka and Purim, on which the Sages instituted giving thanks for the miracle, one adds the \"Al Ha-nissim\" wording in the blessing of the land, which is a blessing of thanksgiving.</p><p>One who began eating Se'uda Shlishit (the third Shabbat meal) before sunset and finished it after the emergence of the stars says \"Retzei Ve-haḥalitzenu\" in Birkat Ha-mazon. For since he began eating on Shabbat, he already became obligated in Birkat Ha-mazon on Shabbat, and consequently became obligated to mention \"Retzei Ve-haḥalitzenu\" in it. The same law applies on other days: if one began the meal at a time when something had to be added in Birkat Ha-mazon, even though he finished his meal after the emergence of the stars, he must add and mention what he was already obligated to add at the beginning of the meal. And likewise the reverse: if one began his meal on an ordinary day and continued his meal after the emergence of the stars on a day that is Rosh Ḥodesh, since he continued to eat a kezayit of bread after the emergence of the stars, he must add \"Ya'aleh Ve-yavo\" in Birkat Ha-mazon.<sup>6</sup></p><p>If Rosh Ḥodesh falls on Motza'ei Shabbat and one continued to eat a kezayit of bread after the emergence of the stars, the poskim disagree about what he should say in Birkat Ha-mazon: whether he should mention only Rosh Ḥodesh (Magen Avraham 188:18), or only Shabbat (Ben Ish Ḥai; Yeḥaveh Da'at 3:55), or mention both, because the time of the meal connects the two times (Taz 7; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 17). Some are accustomed to finish their meal before the emergence of the stars in order to avoid the doubt and mention \"Retzei\" alone. And according to my teacher and master, Rav Tzvi Yehuda Kook ztz\"l, if one wishes to prolong his meal after the emergence of the stars and eats a kezayit of bread then, he should say \"Retzei\" and \"Ya'aleh Ve-yavo\" (Olat Re'iya vol. 1, p. 364).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"According to the Rosh, we go only by the time of the blessing, so that if, for example, one blessed after the emergence of the stars at the end of Shabbat, he does not mention Shabbat. In contrast, according to the Hagahot Maimoniyot in the name of the Maharam, the Maharil and Orḥot Ḥayim, we go by the time of eating, and therefore if one began to eat before sunset, he mentions Shabbat. So too wrote SA 188:10 and many Aḥaronim. And it is implied from this that even if one finished his eating on a day that has an addition, he mentions the addition, and so wrote MB 188:33, and so too SA 271:6 regarding Shabbat. If one prayed Arvit before Birkat Ha-mazon, since he has already begun the new day, he does not mention the addition that belongs to the time when his meal began (Maharil; MB 188:32)."}]},{"slug":"10-04-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-04-08/","title_he":"ח – שכח לומר 'רצה' או 'יעלה ויבוא'","title_en":"One Who Forgot to Say Retzei or Ya'aleh Ve-yavo","body_en":"<p>The law for one who forgot to say \"Retzei\" or \"Ya'aleh Ve-yavo\" in Birkat Ha-mazon depends on the obligation of the meal. If it is a meal at which it is obligatory to eat bread, it follows that he was obligated to recite Birkat Ha-mazon and to mention the day, and since he forgot, he must go back and recite Birkat Ha-mazon again. But if it is a meal at which there is no obligation to eat bread, he does not go back. Therefore, if he forgot to say \"Retzei\" at the first two meals of Shabbat, since he was obligated to eat bread at them, he must recite Birkat Ha-mazon again. But if he forgot to mention \"Retzei\" at Se'uda Shlishit, which after the fact can be fulfilled without bread, or if he forgot to say \"Ya'aleh Ve-yavo\" on Rosh Ḥodesh or Ḥol Ha-mo'ed, on which there is no obligation to eat bread, he need not go back and recite Birkat Ha-mazon again (Berakhot 49b; SA 188:6-8; Peninei Halakha: Shabbat 7:6; Zemanim 1:11).</p><p>The same law applies to the two meals of Yom Tov, at which, according to most Rishonim, it is obligatory to eat bread; therefore, if he did not mention \"Ya'aleh Ve-yavo\" at them, he must go back and recite Birkat Ha-mazon again, and this is the custom of most of Israel. Some have a different custom, as explained in the note.<sup>7</sup></p><p>At meals for which one must go back, even if he has only begun the blessing of Ha-tov Ve-hametiv, he must go back to the beginning of Birkat Ha-mazon in order to say it properly. One should not make do with going back to the beginning of the blessing of \"Raḥem,\" because once he has begun the blessing of Ha-tov Ve-hametiv, he is considered as one who has entirely finished the Torah blessings of Birkat Ha-mazon, and if he did not fulfill his obligation because he forgot to mention what he was obligated to mention, he must go back to their beginning (SA 188:6; BHL s.v. \"la-rosh\").</p><p>If he remembered that he forgot to say \"Retzei\" or \"Ya'aleh Ve-yavo\" before he began the blessing of Ha-tov Ve-hametiv, the Sages instituted for him the wording of a supplementary blessing that he should say before beginning the blessing of Ha-tov Ve-hametiv. And even in a situation where the one who forgot is not obligated to repeat Birkat Ha-mazon, the Sages instituted saying the supplementary blessing. One who does not know the wording of the supplementary blessing does not fulfill his obligation by saying \"Retzei\" or \"Ya'aleh Ve-yavo\" before the blessing of Ha-tov Ve-hametiv, and when the mention is indispensable, he should go back to the beginning (Taz; MB 17; and see BHL).<sup>8</sup></p><p>When he forgot to say \"Retzei\" or \"Ya'aleh Ve-yavo\" and has already begun Ha-tov Ve-hametiv, and according to the law he need not repeat Birkat Ha-mazon, several poskim wrote that it is preferable that he mention what he forgot within the recitation of \"Ha-raḥaman.\" Likewise, one who forgot to say \"Al Ha-nissim,\" whose recitation is not indispensable, should properly say it within the recitation of \"Ha-raḥaman,\" for there a person may add expressions of thanks as he wishes (Rema 682:1; MB 4; Peninei Halakha: Zemanim 11, note 7).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"According to most Rishonim, on every Yom Tov it is obligatory to eat bread at the two meals; therefore, if he forgot to mention \"Ya'aleh Ve-yavo,\" he goes back. This is the view of the Rambam, the Rosh, the Ritva, the Mordekhai, the Ran and many others; so wrote SA 188:6, and so wrote many Aḥaronim. However, according to the Rashba and Tosafot (Sukka 27a), only at the meals of the Seder night and the first night of Sukkot is it obligatory to eat bread, and therefore only at them does one go back. Several of the great Sephardic Aḥaronim wrote that since this is a doubt concerning blessings, and the obligation to mention the festival is rabbinic, one should not go back (Ben Ish Ḥai, Ḥukat 21; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 24; Yeḥaveh Da'at 5:36), and this is the custom of some of those of Sephardic descent. However, the custom of those of Ashkenazic, North African and Yemenite descent follows the SA (Erekh Ha-shulḥan 188:3; Sho'el Ve-nish'al vol. 5, OḤ 83; Shemesh U-magen 1:13; Alei Hadas 10:8; Pe'ulat Tzaddik 3:35). On Rosh Ha-shana there is a doubt, since there were those who were accustomed to fast; therefore he should not go back (MB 188:19; Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 15).<br>The poskim disagree about the law of one who is in doubt whether he said \"Retzei\" or \"Ya'aleh Ve-yavo\" at meals for which one must go back. MB 188:16 ruled that he should go back, since presumably he spoke out of habit without the mention. So too wrote Birkat Hashem vol. 2, 5:18. However, Yabia Omer 7:28 wrote in accordance with the poskim who hold that in a case of doubt he does not go back, since the mention of the day itself is rabbinic, and in a rabbinic doubt we are lenient; all the more so here, where also in a doubt concerning blessings we are lenient. Furthermore, perhaps the holiness of the day caused him to remember. It seems that when it is likely that he forgot, he should go back, but in an evenly balanced doubt he should not go back. Regarding a woman who forgot \"Ya'aleh Ve-yavo\" on Yom Tov, see Peninei Halakha: Mo'adim 2, note 5, that even when she certainly forgot, there is a doubt whether she must go back, and therefore she should not go back, and one who does go back has a basis on which to rely; and on the Seder night she must go back."},{"n":8,"en":"On <b>Shabbat</b> one says: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who gave Sabbaths for rest to His people Israel in love, as a sign and a covenant. Blessed are You, Lord, who sanctifies the Shabbat.\"<br>On <b>Rosh Ḥodesh</b>, according to the Sephardic custom one says: \"Blessed is He who gave New Moons to His people Israel for remembrance,\" and according to the Ashkenazic custom: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who gave New Moons to His people Israel for remembrance\" (SA 188:7; MB 25, and BHL there). If <b>Rosh Ḥodesh falls on Shabbat</b> and one forgot both, he includes Rosh Ḥodesh with Shabbat and says: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who gave Sabbaths for rest to His people Israel in love, as a sign and a covenant, and New Moons for remembrance. Blessed are You, Lord, who sanctifies the Shabbat, Israel and the New Moons\" (SA 188:7; MB 30; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 38).<br>On <b>Yom Tov</b> one says: \"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who gave festivals to Israel for gladness and joy, this day of the Festival of (Pesaḥ / Shavuot / Sukkot). Blessed are You, Lord, who sanctifies Israel and the seasons.\" On <b>Ḥol Ha-mo'ed</b>, instead of \"who gave festivals\" one says \"who gave appointed seasons,\" and one does not conclude with a blessing, as on Rosh Ḥodesh (MB 188:27). And according to the Sephardic custom, one does not even say God's name and kingship there.<br>If <b>Yom Tov falls on Shabbat</b> and one forgot to say both, he says: \"Blessed … who gave Sabbaths for rest to His people Israel in love, as a sign and a covenant, and festivals for gladness and joy, this day of the Festival of (Pesaḥ / Shavuot / Sukkot). Blessed are You, Lord, who sanctifies the Shabbat, Israel and the seasons.\"<br>If he remembered that he forgot to say \"Retzei\" or \"Ya'aleh Ve-yavo\" before he said God's name at the end of Boneh Yerushalayim, he should go back and complete what he omitted. If he already said God's name, according to many he should conclude \"lamdeini ḥukekha\" (\"teach me Your statutes\"), for otherwise he will have to add the supplementary blessing. If he already said \"Boneh,\" he should finish the blessing and say the supplementary blessing. If he began the \"Barukh\" of Ha-tov Ve-hametiv, according to most poskim he has lost the possibility of saying the supplementary blessing (MB 188:23; Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 18; BHL s.v. \"ad\"). Some say that if he is in a situation where otherwise he would have to recite Birkat Ha-mazon again, as long as he is still saying the opening words of the blessing, which are identical to the supplementary blessing, he should continue into the supplementary blessing (Ḥayei Adam; Yabia Omer 6:28)."}]},{"slug":"10-04-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-04-09/","title_he":"ט – מים אחרונים","title_en":"Mayim Aḥaronim","body_en":"<p>The Sages said that it is obligatory to wash the hands before Birkat Ha-mazon, and this washing is called \"mayim aḥaronim\" (\"final waters\"). The reason for the washing is that they used Sodomite salt, which could blind the eyes (Ḥullin 105a-b). They further said that there is a mitzva to wash the hands before Birkat Ha-mazon in order to sanctify oneself for the blessing, for it is not fitting to say Birkat Ha-mazon when the hands are soiled with food remnants (Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona and the Rosh, based on Berakhot 53b). And although it is obligatory to wash mayim aḥaronim, no blessing is recited over this washing, since the main purpose of the enactment is to prevent danger, and one does not recite a blessing over the removal of danger.</p><p>According to the Tosafists, there is no obligation today to wash mayim aḥaronim, because we do not use salt that endangers the eyesight. Furthermore, several of the great Aḥaronim wrote that there is not even a mitzva in it, because today, when we eat with cutlery, the hands do not become soiled from the food, and therefore there is no need to clean them in preparation for Birkat Ha-mazon.</p><p>In practice, although the view of most poskim is that even in our times one must wash mayim aḥaronim, many are accustomed to be lenient, among them Torah scholars and righteous women. This is because the law of mayim aḥaronim is rabbinic, and the rule is that in a dispute concerning a rabbinic law, the halakha follows the lenient view. However, one whose hands are soiled and who intends to rinse them anyway should rinse them before Birkat Ha-mazon (SA 181:1, 10).</p><p>The Kabbalists added a reason according to the esoteric teaching: through mayim aḥaronim one removes the impurity of the food that has stuck to the hands, which is the portion of the sitra aḥra (the \"other side,\" the forces of impurity), and by removing it a person is protected from accusation (Zohar vol. 2, 154b; Zohar Ḥadash, Rut 106b). Therefore those who conduct themselves according to Kabbala are meticulous about washing mayim aḥaronim, but women did not adopt this Kabbalistic custom, and therefore in many families only the men are meticulous about washing mayim aḥaronim.<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"According to Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona and the Rosh, it is obligatory to wash mayim aḥaronim because of the danger of Sodomite salt, as cited in Ḥullin 105a: \"The first waters are a mitzva; the final waters are an obligation.\" In addition, since the hands are soiled from the food, there is a mitzva to wash mayim aḥaronim in honor of the blessing, as cited in Berakhot 53b: \"It was taught: 'You shall sanctify yourselves' – this refers to the first waters; 'and you shall be holy' – this refers to the final waters; 'for I am holy' – this is the fragrant oil (which contained spices, and which holds back the blessing for one who is accustomed to it – Rashi); 'I am the Lord your God' – this is the blessing.\" According to the Rambam (Hilkhot Berakhot 6:3), the reason for the washing is danger, and with other salt as well one must be concerned that perhaps it will cause harm like Sodomite salt. According to the Rama\"h, one recites a blessing over washing them, and according to the Behag one does not recite a blessing, for the reason cited above (Tur 181:7).<br>But Tosafot (Ḥullin 105a) wrote that people are not accustomed to wash mayim aḥaronim, because there is no Sodomite salt among us, and the verse cited in Berakhot is only an asmakhta (a scriptural allusion), as evidenced by the fact that people today do not observe the practice of the oil. End of quotation. And even if we say that in honor of the blessing one must remove the dirt from the hands, it can be said that this was so when people were accustomed to eat with their hands, without cutlery, even moist and sticky foods, and therefore they were also accustomed to anoint their hands with perfumed oil, to remove the smell of the cooked dishes from them. But we, who eat with cutlery, need neither mayim aḥaronim nor fragrant oil (the Mor U-ketzi'a wrote along these lines).<br>SA 181:1 wrote that it is obligatory to wash mayim aḥaronim, and in se'if 10 mentioned that some are accustomed to be lenient. So too many poskim wrote that it is proper to be stringent, although they mentioned the view of those who are lenient (Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 9). And some wrote explicitly to be stringent (Gra; Birkei Yosef; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 5; Ben Ish Ḥai, Shelaḥ 6). Women are obligated in this like men, and so wrote Halikhot Olam vol. 2, Shelaḥ Lekha 1, and Responsa Ma'amar Mordekhai vol. 2, OḤ 18. So too wrote Halikhot Beita 12:2 and Halikhot Bat Yisrael 3:5, but they noted that in practice women have not been accustomed to wash. See further the words of the Kabbalists in Kaf Ha-ḥayim 181:1 and 27.<br>In practice, it seems that according to the law every person may be lenient in this. There are two main reasons why the halakha follows the lenient view: 1) It is a doubt concerning a rabbinic law, and although the stringent authorities are more numerous, the reasoning of the lenient ones is strong. 2) This is the prevalent custom, as evidenced by the fact that for many generations most women, including God-fearing ones, were not accustomed to wash mayim aḥaronim, and custom has great force. Along these lines, Shevet Ha-Levi 4:23 wrote that washing mayim aḥaronim today is a stringency, and women did not accept it upon themselves. And Piskei Teshuvot 181:1 wrote that people became accustomed to be stringent and wash mayim aḥaronim according to Kabbala, and women are not particular about Kabbalistic customs. In summary: the main halakhic position is that mayim aḥaronim today are not an obligation but a good custom; blessing will come upon one who is stringent and observes it, and it is preferable that he not accept it upon himself as a binding custom and a vow."}]},{"slug":"10-04-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-04-10/","title_he":"י – דיני מים אחרונים","title_en":"Laws of Mayim Aḥaronim","body_en":"<p>For those who are accustomed to wash mayim aḥaronim, one need wash only two joints of the fingers, and for the thumb one joint suffices, because the dirt of the food does not reach beyond that (SA 181:4). Some are meticulous to wash the entire fingers (BHL s.v. \"ad\"; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 15-16).</p><p>None of the laws that are indispensable for the washing before the meal apply to washing mayim aḥaronim; therefore there is no need to wash the hands from a vessel, but one may pour water over them directly from the tap, and many are accustomed to wash their hands from the tap into the sink. There is no minimum measure of water; the main thing is that the dirt be cleaned from the two joints of the fingers.<sup>10</sup></p><p>One should not wash with very hot water, because when one washes with a little water, as people are accustomed to, it makes the dirt absorb into the hands (see SA 181:3).</p><p>When washing, one must lower the fingers, so that the water will run down and remove the dirt from the fingers (Sota 4b; SA 181:5). It is proper to dry the fingers before beginning to bless (SA 181:8; MB 19).</p><p>Mayim aḥaronim are not washed onto the ground, because the Sages said that they contain an evil spirit that can cause harm. Therefore, when one does not wash them into a sink, one must wash them into a vessel, and afterward pour the water into a sink or a place where people do not walk. It is customary, ideally, to remove the vessel from the table or to cover it before the blessing.<sup>11</sup></p><p>After washing mayim aḥaronim, one should hasten to recite Birkat Ha-mazon, for the Sages said (Berakhot 42a): \"Immediately following the washing of the hands comes the blessing.\" Ideally, one should not interrupt by speaking, even with words of Torah, between the washing and the blessing. Nor should one needlessly walk more than the distance of 22 amot, or pause in silence for the length of time it takes to walk 22 amot (see MB 179:1-2, BHL there, and 181:15 and 24).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"So wrote BY 181 and MB, se'if katan 19 and 21, and Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 31, in the name of the Eliya Rabba and other Aḥaronim, and not in accordance with the view of the Shiltei Ha-gibborim, who requires a vessel and the other things that are indispensable for the washing before the meal. And indeed MB 10 wrote that one should not make do with a few drops, as some are accustomed, but rather the first two joints of the fingers must be cleaned; however, many are accustomed to wash with a little water, because today the fingers are clean, and the washing is observed only according to Kabbala, and in order not to increase the portion of the sitra aḥra, they limit the water to what will wet the fingers, even though it does not have the power to clean the fingers of dirt. (See the Gra, who wrote to wash with a revi'it, like the law of the first waters; in contrast, many wrote to use little water for mayim aḥaronim, and so wrote the Ben Ish Ḥai, Shelaḥ 9, and Kaf Ha-ḥayim 6; see Piskei Teshuvot 9.)"},{"n":11,"en":"See Ḥullin 105a; SA 181:2. Yabia Omer 5:2 explained that it is permitted to wash into a sink. See BHL s.v. \"ela bi-khli,\" that there are differing views as to whether, after washing into a vessel, an evil spirit remains on the water, and on this depends the question of whether one must be careful to pour the water in the vessel into a sink or the like. And Rabbi Ḥayim Palagi wrote that it is good to remove the vessel from before oneself at the time of the blessing (Kaf Ha-ḥayim 8)."}]},{"slug":"10-04-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-04-11/","title_he":"יא – מנהגים בברכת המזון","title_en":"Customs in Birkat Ha-mazon","body_en":"<p>It is proper not to remove the tablecloth and the bread from the table until after Birkat Ha-mazon, for by leaving bread on the table one shows that God has given food in abundance, and thereby the thanks and praise in the blessing are said in completeness. In addition, a person should delay clearing away the bread, so that if a poor person happens to come to him, he will be able to break off some of his bread for him. The Sages said that whoever does not leave bread on his table at the time of the blessing does not see a sign of blessing. But if he prepares himself to give of his bread to the poor, God too will continue to grant him of His kindness. In addition, when a person leaves over some of his bread, he shows that he is happy with his lot and is even prepared to make do with less than what God has given him, and such a person is worthy of blessing. But if he finishes everything, it implies that he is not happy with his lot, and whatever he is given he will finish and will still want to eat more, and such a man is not worthy of blessing.</p><p>If they finished all the bread at the meal, it is good to bring bread to lie on the table at the time of the blessing. But if bread remains there, they should not bring more bread, because this is how the gentiles were accustomed to place bread after their meal in honor of their idolatry. It is preferable that from the outset there be plenty of bread on the table, so that some of it will remain until after the blessing (SA 180:1-2; Taz 1; MB 5).</p><p>One who eats away from home, and if he leaves remnants of bread he will later have to throw them away, should leave over only small crumbs, which fall anyway while he eats, and he should not eat them, so that he will see himself as having eaten and left over.<sup>12</sup></p><p>In addition to the bread, it is customary to leave the salt on the table at the time of the blessing as well; however, regarding the remnants of other foods and the dirty dishes, there is no halakha. Some are meticulous to remove them from the table, so that the table will be clean in honor of Birkat Ha-mazon (Levush; and see Ben Ish Ḥai, Shelaḥ 2).</p><p>Several of the Rishonim wrote that it is customary to cover the sharp knife or to remove it from the table at the time of Birkat Ha-mazon. For the table resembles the altar, as the Sages said (Ḥagiga 27a) that now that a person has no altar on which to offer sacrifices to atone for his soul, his table, at which he hosts guests and the poor, atones for him. And just as we were commanded not to wield a sword over the stones of the altar (Shemot 20:22), because it is not fitting to wield the sword, which shortens a person's days, over the altar, which lengthens a person's days, so it is not fitting to place a sharp knife, which can shorten a person's days, on the table that is meant to add life, upon which we eat and bless. This custom was observed specifically at the time of the blessing, which is a time of kindness, whereas the knife alludes to judgment. However, on Shabbat and Yom Tov many are not particular about this, because they are days of holiness and blessing, and there is no need to be concerned on them about a bad sign (see BY, SA 180:5; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 6).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"In Sanhedrin 92a and in the Zohar (vol. 1, 88; vol. 2, 86b; 157b), the matter of the blessing in leaving over bread is cited. See Piskei Teshuvot 180, note 13, that the Ḥatam Sofer would eat the crumbs of bread left over from breaking the bread at Ha-motzi as a segula (spiritual remedy) for memory, and the book Yesod Yosef, os 22, wrote in the name of the Kabbalist sages that it is a segula for rectifying the sin of nocturnal emission. It seems to me in my humble opinion that when a person leaves over from his food whole slices, or a whole loaf or roll, then there is an enhancement of the mitzva in eating the crumbs so as not to destroy them. And when he has no slice or roll left, he should leave the crumbs, and through them the blessing of having eaten and left over will be expressed, and afterward he should clear the crumbs away in a respectful manner."}]},{"slug":"10-04-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-04-12/","title_he":"יב – דרך אמירת ברכת המזון וכבודה","title_en":"The Manner of Reciting Birkat Ha-mazon and Its Dignity","body_en":"<p>One must sit at the time of the blessing, for this makes it easier to concentrate. Even one who was eating while standing or while walking about his house must sit for Birkat Ha-mazon. He should not recline in a manner of arrogance and levity, but rather sit in a dignified manner. After the fact, if he blessed while standing, walking or reclining, he has fulfilled his obligation (SA 183:9).</p><p>Some are meticulous to wear a jacket and put on a hat in honor of Birkat Ha-mazon (MB 183:11). But for one who is not accustomed to go about in a jacket and hat, there is no enhancement of the mitzva in this.</p><p>It is forbidden for a person to do work at the time of the blessing, even a slight action such as arranging the plate in front of him, so that he will not divert his attention from the blessing. In addition, one who busies himself with his hands during the blessing shows that he is not concentrating, harms the honor of Heaven and disgraces the blessing (SA 183:12; 191:3).</p><p>All the more so it is forbidden to interrupt by speaking in the middle of Birkat Ha-mazon. Some say that one who deliberately interrupts by speaking in the middle of Birkat Ha-mazon has lost all that he said and must begin Birkat Ha-mazon again from the beginning (Magen Avraham). However, in practice, after the fact, if he interrupted by speaking in the middle of Birkat Ha-mazon, he should continue from where he stopped and finish the blessing (MB 183:25).</p><p>Likewise, it is forbidden for a person to signal something to his friend at the time of the blessing, and if there is something there that disturbs his concentration, he should stop blessing and signal to his friend to remove it. And if a Torah scholar enters, it is permitted to rise in his honor.<sup>13</sup></p><p>One who was eating while walking because he was hurrying on his way may also recite Birkat Ha-mazon while walking, because if he stops he will be preoccupied with the loss of time and will not be able to concentrate properly (SA 183:11; MB 36). Likewise one who is driving a car, if he is an experienced driver who is not preoccupied by the road, may in case of need bless while driving (see Sha'arei Teshuva 63:4). And the other passengers in the vehicle may bless ideally during the journey.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":13,"en":"Although it was permitted to interrupt the recitation of Shema to greet someone out of fear or respect, in Birkat Ha-mazon one does not interrupt, for in this respect its law is like that of the Amida prayer (SA 183:8; and see Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 11; MB 30). Likewise one should not interrupt for Kaddish and Kedusha (Kaf Ha-ḥayim 183:45; Yabia Omer vol. 1, OḤ 11), and some say that it is permitted to interrupt, as in the blessings of Shema (see Arukh Ha-shulḥan 183:8). But once he has reached \"Ha-raḥaman,\" which is not part of the essence of Birkat Ha-mazon, it is permitted in case of need to interrupt by speaking. Likewise, in a pressing situation, one who is in a hurry and needs to set out on his way may begin walking after finishing the fourth blessing, and continue saying from \"Ha-raḥaman\" onward while walking."}]},{"slug":"10-04-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-04-13/","title_he":"יג – ברכה במקום אכילתו (איזה מאכלים טעונים ברכה במקומם)","title_en":"Blessing in the Place Where One Ate (Which Foods Require a Blessing in Their Place)","body_en":"<p>Because of the importance of Birkat Ha-mazon, the Sages established that it be said in the place of the meal. Ideally, one should bless in the place where one sat while eating, and in case of need, as long as he is in the same room, he is considered as blessing in the place where he ate.<sup>14</sup> Ideally, it is forbidden for a person to leave the place of his meal before reciting Birkat Ha-mazon, even if he intends to return.</p><p>If he forgot to bless and went on his way, or mistakenly thought that one may bless in another place, even if he traveled a great distance, he must return to the place of his meal in order to recite Birkat Ha-mazon. If he has a little bread where he is, instead of returning to the place of his meal he may wash his hands, recite Ha-motzi and eat a little of the bread, and afterward recite Birkat Ha-mazon; since he ate bread there too, it turns out that he blessed in the place where he ate. In a pressing situation, when he has no bread there and it is very difficult for him to return to the place of his meal, it is permitted to bless not in the place of eating (SA 184:1-2).<sup>15</sup></p><p>Some say that only bread requires a blessing in its place (Rashba). According to many, all five species of grain require a blessing in their place, for whoever is obligated in the blessing of Al Ha-miḥya is obligated to recite it in the place where he ate (Rif, Tosafot and the Rosh). And some are stringent regarding all seven species, that whoever is obligated in the Me'ein Shalosh blessing must recite it in the place where he ate (Rambam).</p><p>Ideally, the blessing of Borei Nefashot too should be recited in the place of eating, for if he leaves there is a concern that he will forget to bless (MB 178:36). But if he left, he need not return, and likewise one who is in a hurry to leave may recite Borei Nefashot in another place. But with the seven species, and especially with the five species of grain, it is proper, when possible, to be stringent and return, in order to recite the after-blessing in the place where he ate.<sup>16</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":14,"en":"Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 178:4; and see above 3:11 regarding when it is permitted ideally to continue the meal in another place. Regarding the definition of the place of eating, many wrote that as long as he is in the same room he may ideally bless (Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 184, end of se'if 1; MB 1; Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 5; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 1). However, from Ḥayei Adam 47:5-6 it is implied that this is only when there is some need, and so wrote Kaf Ha-ḥayim 1, that one should not change place without a reason. According to all views, in case of need one may bless in another room in the same house if he can see the place where he ate, or when he intended from the outset to bless in another room, even though he cannot see the place where he ate."},{"n":15,"en":"When he has bread in the place he has reached, he need not return to the place of his meal, but he must recite a blessing over the additional bread, because he diverted his mind from continuing to eat. If he did not divert his mind, he should eat without a blessing, and afterward recite Birkat Ha-mazon there. The basis of the discussion is the dispute between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel in Berakhot 53b. According to Beit Shammai, one who inadvertently leaves the place of his meal without blessing must return to his place to bless, and according to Beit Hillel he need not. According to Rav Amram Gaon and the Rosh, the halakha follows Beit Shammai. According to the Rambam and Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona, the halakha follows Beit Hillel, except that even according to Beit Hillel, if he returns to bless in his place, this is praiseworthy. SA 184:1 mentioned both views without a clear ruling, and I have written in accordance with the Aḥaronim (MB 7), which is a middle position.<br><b>If he left deliberately</b>, even if it is very difficult for him, according to all views he must return to his place and bless. After the fact, if he blessed in another place, he has fulfilled his obligation (MB 5, even according to the Rosh and not like the Tur). If he has a little bread there, he may eat it, and this helps ideally even for one who left deliberately (MB 8). If he has no bread there, and by the time he returns the food in his stomach will be digested and he will no longer be able to recite Birkat Ha-mazon, then in order not to lose the blessing he should bless in the place where he is (Magen Avraham 2; MB 3)."},{"n":16,"en":"The three views are mentioned in SA 178:5 and 184:3. In practice, the Ben Ish Ḥai (Be-ha'alotkha 6) was entirely stringent, that even if one left his place inadvertently he must return for all seven species. According to Halikhot Olam (vol. 2, Be-ha'alotkha 4, in the note), even for mezonot one need not return to his place, because it is a double doubt (sfek sfeika): perhaps the halakha follows Beit Hillel, that one need not return, and perhaps the halakha follows the Rashba, that only bread requires a blessing in its place. MB 178:45 wrote a middle view, that ideally it is proper to be stringent with mezonot and the seven species and bless in the place of eating."}]},{"slug":"10-04-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-04-14/","title_he":"יד – עד אימתי יכול לברך","title_en":"Until When One May Bless","body_en":"<p>It is permitted to hold a meal for many hours, to pause between one course and the next for words of Torah and conversation, to sing and dance and continue eating, for as long as he still intends to continue his meal, the first blessing covers what he is about to eat, and Birkat Ha-mazon will cover all that he ate. Ideally, in order to maintain the continuity of the meal, it is preferable that 72 minutes not pass in which he neither eats nor drinks.</p><p>After he finishes his meal, he should hasten to recite Birkat Ha-mazon and not occupy himself with anything else before he blesses.</p><p>After the fact, if he did not bless immediately, he may bless as long as the enjoyment of what he ate continues, that is, as long as he still feels satiated from what he ate. If he became hungry, to the point that he began to want to eat another meal, he has lost the blessing. Generally, people do not feel hungry after a meal for several hours. If he ate light foods at the meal, the feeling of satiety lasts about two or three hours, and if he ate heavy foods, he will feel satiated for up to six hours. If he forgot to bless after the meal, and several hours have passed, and he is in doubt whether he has begun to be hungry, he should recite Birkat Ha-mazon, for since he was initially satisfied and became obligated in Birkat Ha-mazon by Torah law, in a situation of doubt he is obligated to bless. When it is clear to him that he has begun to be hungry again, or that six hours have passed, he has lost Birkat Ha-mazon.</p><p>If he ate a little bread and was not satisfied, as long as he still enjoys what he ate, that is, he still feels more satiated than he was before he began to eat, he may still recite Birkat Ha-mazon. This is difficult to estimate, and therefore a person who ate less than enough to be satisfied must hasten to bless immediately after finishing eating. After the fact, if about an hour has passed without his having blessed, ideally he should eat another kezayit, and in this way he will be able to recite Birkat Ha-mazon according to all views. If he does not have another kezayit there, as long as 72 minutes have not passed since he finished eating, he may still bless, and after that he should not bless. Only if it is clear to him that he is still more satiated than he was before he ate may he bless even after 72 minutes.<sup>17</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":17,"en":"Mishna Berakhot 51b: \"Until when may one bless? Until the food in his stomach is digested.\" And in the Gemara (53b): \"How long is the period of digestion? Rabbi Yoḥanan said: As long as he is not hungry.\" Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona explained that the meaning is until he begins to be hungry, even though the food has not yet been completely digested. So too wrote SA 184:5.<br>One who eats a satisfying meal remains satiated for several hours, depending on the type of foods and on the person. However, after six hours the period of digestion has certainly passed, and he should not bless (Magen Avraham 184:9). One who holds a meal lasting hours, even if the period of digestion has passed since he ate the bread, since he ate more foods and drank more beverages and remains satisfied, he may still recite Birkat Ha-mazon, for the entire meal with all its components is considered a single unit (Magen Avraham 184:9; MB 18; and this is also the view of the overwhelming majority of poskim). Ideally, it is good that 72 minutes not pass without his tasting something (Kaf Ha-ḥayim 28-29. According to Kitzur Shulḥan Arukh 44:8, it is good to eat specifically bread within each interval of less than 72 minutes, for ideally one should not rely on the reasoning of the Magen Avraham).<br>If he ate an amount that does not satisfy, it is difficult to estimate, since immediately after he finishes eating he remains hungry, and sometimes it is precisely through eating that one feels hungrier. The view of most poskim is that up to the time it takes to walk 4 mil (72 minutes) he may certainly bless, for even Reish Lakish, who disagrees with Rabbi Yoḥanan and shortens the time during which one may still bless after a large amount of eating, agrees that after a small amount of eating one may bless up to this measure. So wrote the Taz, the Eliya Rabba, the Magen Giborim, and MB 20, and BHL there. However, according to the Magen Avraham 9, the only criterion is that he feel more satiated than he was before he began to eat, and since it is difficult to estimate this measure, in any situation of doubt one should eat another kezayit before reciting the after-blessing, or fulfill his obligation by hearing the blessing from his friend. And when he cannot make use of these solutions, according to the Magen Avraham he should not bless. However, according to the other poskim he may bless in any case up to 72 minutes, and this is the halakha.<br>It should be noted that with a small amount of eating, the measure is that he still feels more satiated than he was before he began to eat, whereas with a large amount of eating, even if he feels more satiated than he was before the meal, once he has begun to be hungry and to want to eat a meal again, he has lost the blessing. Perhaps it can be explained that with a small amount of eating, we are dealing with a short time, and therefore one can assess whether he is still more satiated than he was before he ate. But with satisfying eating, a long time passes, and the satiety and the time make him forget the feeling of hunger that he had before the previous meal; therefore, if he has begun to want to eat again, he can no longer bless. Below (10:12, note 15) this law is cited regarding other foods."}]}]});
